{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WgD6wqYnbh3gWxKDJh37K9","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd021-tac\n     2→title: \"cmd/tac: Concatenate and Print Files in Reverse\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  tac is the reverse of cat: it prints lines of its input in reverse order. macOS does not\n     6→  ship a native tac equivalent; scripts that use tac depend on the Homebrew GNU binary\n     7→  (gtac, installed by coreutils). The Go implementation must match gtac byte-for-byte on\n     8→  all flag and input combinations, including custom separators and binary input, verified\n     9→  by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n    10→\n    11→goals:\n    12→  - G1: Read each file (or stdin) and write its records in reverse order, matching GNU tac behavior.\n    13→  - G2: Support custom record separators via -s, enabling reversal on boundaries other than newlines.\n    14→  - G3: Support -b to position the separator before the record rather than after it.\n    15→  - G4: Support -r to interpret the separator as a regular expression.\n    16→\n    17→requirements:\n    18→  R1:\n    19→    title: Core reversal behavior\n    20→    items:\n    21→      - R1.1: Must read the entire input into memory, split it into records on the separator (default newline), and write the records in reverse order to stdout.\n    22→      - R1.2: Must treat a trailing separator at the end of input as the terminator of the last record, not as a separator before an empty record. This matches gtac behavior where \"a\\nb\\n\" reversed produces \"b\\na\\n\" not \"\\nb\\na\".\n    23→      - R1.3: Must read from stdin when no file arguments are given, or when \"-\" appears as a filename.\n    24→      - R1.4: When multiple files are given, must process each file independently and write them in argument order (each file's records reversed, files not merged before reversal).\n    25→\n    26→  R2:\n    27→    title: Separator options (-s, -b, -r)\n    28→    items:\n    29→      - R2.1: -s SEP must use SEP as the record separator instead of newline. SEP may be any string.\n    30→      - R2.2: By default the separator follows each record. -b must place the separator before each record instead of after it.\n    31→      - R2.3: -r must interpret the -s SEP argument as a regular expression (Go regexp syntax). Each match of the pattern becomes a record boundary.\n    32→      - R2.4: When -s and -r are combined, the separator is a regex; records are the text between consecutive matches.\n    33→\n    34→  R3:\n    35→    title: Exit codes and SIGPIPE\n    36→    items:\n    37→      - R3.1: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n    38→      - R3.2: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened or read, printing the error to stderr. Processing continues for remaining files.\n    39→      - R3.3: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n    40→      - R3.4: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully (exit 0) using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler.\n    41→\n    42→  R4:\n    43→    title: Differential testing\n    44→    items:\n    45→      - R4.1: Differential tests must compare Go tac output against gtac byte-for-byte via pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests.\n    46→      - R4.2: \"Tests must cover: single-file reversal, stdin reversal, multi-file reversal, -s with a custom separator, -b flag, and a file with no trailing newline.\"\n    47→      - R4.3: Tests must use LC_ALL=C to eliminate locale-dependent divergence.\n    48→\n    49→non_goals:\n    50→  - cmd/tac does not implement the block-level backward seek optimization from the C source; it buffers the full input in memory.\n    51→  - cmd/tac does not support files too large to fit in memory; it accumulates all input before producing output.\n    52→\n    53→acceptance_criteria:\n    54→  - \"AC1: printf 'a\\\\nb\\\\nc\\\\n' | tac produces 'c\\\\nb\\\\na\\\\n', matching gtac byte-for-byte.\"\n    55→  - \"AC2: printf 'a\\\\nb\\\\nc' | tac produces 'c\\\\nb\\\\na' (no trailing newline), matching gtac.\"\n    56→  - \"AC3: tac -s : on 'a:b:c:' produces 'c:b:a:', matching gtac -s :.\"\n    57→  - \"AC4: tac -b -s : on ':a:b:c' produces ':c:b:a', matching gtac -b -s :.\"\n    58→  - \"AC5: Differential test against gtac passes for all required test cases.\"\n    59→  - \"AC6: cmd/tac compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    60→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"abb2040a-f7c9-438c-b4c3-2eddab19244e","uuid":"5ddec3ee-2df4-415f-84e8-58ec30ffa788","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/347/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd021-tac.yaml","content":"id: prd021-tac\ntitle: \"cmd/tac: Concatenate and Print Files in Reverse\"\n\nproblem: |\n  tac is the reverse of cat: it prints lines of its input in reverse order. macOS does not\n  ship a native tac equivalent; scripts that use tac depend on the Homebrew GNU binary\n  (gtac, installed by coreutils). The Go implementation must match gtac byte-for-byte on\n  all flag and input combinations, including custom separators and binary input, verified\n  by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Read each file (or stdin) and write its records in reverse order, matching GNU tac behavior.\n  - G2: Support custom record separators via -s, enabling reversal on boundaries other than newlines.\n  - G3: Support -b to position the separator before the record rather than after it.\n  - G4: Support -r to interpret the separator as a regular expression.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Core reversal behavior\n    items:\n      - R1.1: Must read the entire input into memory, split it into records on the separator (default newline), and write the records in reverse order to stdout.\n      - R1.2: Must treat a trailing separator at the end of input as the terminator of the last record, not as a separator before an empty record. This matches gtac behavior where \"a\\nb\\n\" reversed produces \"b\\na\\n\" not \"\\nb\\na\".\n      - R1.3: Must read from stdin when no file arguments are given, or when \"-\" appears as a filename.\n      - R1.4: When multiple files are given, must process each file independently and write them in argument order (each file's records reversed, files not merged before reversal).\n\n  R2:\n    title: Separator options (-s, -b, -r)\n    items:\n      - R2.1: -s SEP must use SEP as the record separator instead of newline. SEP may be any string.\n      - R2.2: By default the separator follows each record. -b must place the separator before each record instead of after it.\n      - R2.3: -r must interpret the -s SEP argument as a regular expression (Go regexp syntax). Each match of the pattern becomes a record boundary.\n      - R2.4: When -s and -r are combined, the separator is a regex; records are the text between consecutive matches.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Exit codes and SIGPIPE\n    items:\n      - R3.1: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n      - R3.2: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened or read, printing the error to stderr. Processing continues for remaining files.\n      - R3.3: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n      - R3.4: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully (exit 0) using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Differential testing\n    items:\n      - R4.1: Differential tests must compare Go tac output against gtac byte-for-byte via pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests.\n      - R4.2: \"Tests must cover: single-file reversal, stdin reversal, multi-file reversal, -s with a custom separator, -b flag, and a file with no trailing newline.\"\n      - R4.3: Tests must use LC_ALL=C to eliminate locale-dependent divergence.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/tac does not implement the block-level backward seek optimization from the C source; it buffers the full input in memory.\n  - cmd/tac does not support files too large to fit in memory; it accumulates all input before producing output.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: printf 'a\\\\nb\\\\nc\\\\n' | tac produces 'c\\\\nb\\\\na\\\\n', matching gtac byte-for-byte.\"\n  - \"AC2: printf 'a\\\\nb\\\\nc' | tac produces 'c\\\\nb\\\\na' (no trailing newline), matching gtac.\"\n  - \"AC3: tac -s : on 'a:b:c:' produces 'c:b:a:', matching gtac -s :.\"\n  - \"AC4: tac -b -s : on ':a:b:c' produces ':c:b:a', matching gtac -b -s :.\"\n  - \"AC5: Differential test against gtac passes for all required test cases.\"\n  - \"AC6: cmd/tac compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":60,"startLine":1,"totalLines":60}}}
